<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-transpose" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Matrix Transposition</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Transposing a matrix interchanges its rows and columns. This simple operation
      interacts with addition, scalar multiplication, and multiplication in ways that
      we will use repeatedly.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-transpose-definition">
    <title>The Transpose</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-transpose">
      <title>Transpose</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> is an <m>m \times n</m> matrix, then its <term>transpose</term>,
          denoted <m>A^T</m>, is the <m>n \times m</m> matrix whose <m>i</m>th row is the
          <m>i</m>th column of <m>A</m>. That is, if
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} a_{ij} \end{bmatrix}</m>, then
          <md>
            A^T = \begin{bmatrix} a_{ij} \end{bmatrix}^T = \begin{bmatrix} a_{ji} \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          i.e., the <m>(i,j)</m>-entry of <m>A^T</m> is the <m>(j,i)</m>-entry of <m>A</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-transpose">
      <title>Transposing a Matrix</title>
      <p>
        If
        <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}_{3 \times 2}</m>,
        then
        <md>
          A^T = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}_{2 \times 3}.
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-transpose-properties">
      <title>Properties of the Transpose of a Matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be <m>m \times n</m> matrices, <m>C</m> be an
          <m>n \times p</m> matrix, and <m>r \in \R</m> a scalar. Then
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p><m>\left( A^T \right)^T = A</m>.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>(rA)^T = rA^T</m>.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>(A + B)^T = A^T + B^T</m>.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>(AC)^T = C^T A^T</m>.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <remark xml:id="rem-transpose-reverses-order">
      <p>
        Property (4) deserves attention: transposing a product <em>reverses the order</em>
        of the factors. Note also that the sizes work out — <m>C^T</m> is
        <m>p \times n</m> and <m>A^T</m> is <m>n \times m</m>, so <m>C^T A^T</m> is
        <m>p \times m</m>, which is exactly the size of <m>(AC)^T</m>.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-symmetric-matrices">
    <title>Symmetric Matrices</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-symmetric">
      <title>Symmetric Matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A matrix <m>A</m> is called <term>symmetric</term> if and only if
          <m>A^T = A</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-symmetric-combination">
      <title>A Symmetric Combination</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that if <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are symmetric, then <m>A^T + 2B</m> is symmetric.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are symmetric, then <m>A = A^T</m> and <m>B = B^T</m>.
          Using <xref ref="thm-transpose-properties" text="type-global" />,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left( A^T + 2B \right)^T
              \amp = \left( A^T \right)^T + (2B)^T \amp \amp \text{(property 3)}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = A + 2B^T \amp \amp \text{(properties 1 and 2)}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = A^T + 2B \amp \amp (A, B \text{ are symmetric)}
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Since <m>\left( A^T + 2B \right)^T = A^T + 2B</m>, we know that <m>A^T + 2B</m>
          is symmetric by definition.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="rem-is-ab-symmetric">
      <title>Question</title>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be symmetric matrices. Is <m>AB</m> symmetric?
      </p>
      <p>
        Property (4) of <xref ref="thm-transpose-properties" text="type-global" /> gives
        <md>
          (AB)^T = B^T A^T = BA,
        </md>
        so <m>AB</m> is symmetric exactly when <m>BA = AB</m> <mdash /> that is, exactly
        when <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> commute. Since matrix multiplication is not commutative
        in general, the product of two symmetric matrices need not be symmetric.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-transpose-equations">
    <title>Solving Equations Involving the Transpose</title>

    <example xml:id="eg-solve-for-a">
      <title>Solving for an Unknown Matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the matrix <m>A</m> if
          <md>
            \left( A + 3 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 2 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix} \right)^T
            = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 5 \\ 3 \amp 8 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Transpose both sides of the equation. By property (1) of
          <xref ref="thm-transpose-properties" text="type-global" />, the left-hand side
          becomes the matrix itself:
          <md>
            \left( \left( A + 3 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 2 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix} \right)^T \right)^T
            = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 5 \\ 3 \amp 8 \end{bmatrix}^T,
          </md>
          that is,
          <md>
            A + 3 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 2 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\ 1 \amp 5 \amp 8 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Now solve for <m>A</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              A \amp = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\ 1 \amp 5 \amp 8 \end{bmatrix}
                  - \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp -3 \amp 0 \\ 3 \amp 6 \amp 12 \end{bmatrix}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 3 \amp 3 \\ -2 \amp -1 \amp -4 \end{bmatrix}.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
